<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#penal-transportation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penal transportation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penal transportation was the punishment of sending convicted criminals to a distant colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> transported about 50,000 convicts to its American colonies before 1776 and about 162,000 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> between 1788 and 1868, mainly to New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, Van Diemen's Land and later Western Australia. Convict labour built much of early colonial Australia until opposition from free settlers brought the system to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penal-transportation.jpg" alt="Penal transportation" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sayer, Robert, 1725–1794 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-eyed_Sue_and_Sweet_Poll_of_Plymouth_taking_leave_of_their_lovers_who_are_going_to_Botany_Bay.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
